course: biology
lesson: 288
title: Plants we eat: roots, stems, leaves, flowers, fruits and seeds
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A dinner plate is a plant puzzle. The carrot is a root, the celery is a stem, the lettuce is a leaf, the broccoli is a bunch of flower buds, and the peas are seeds.
explain: Scientists and cooks use words differently. To a botanist a fruit is the part of a plant that holds the seeds, so a tomato, a cucumber and a pumpkin are fruits, even if nobody puts them in a fruit salad.
explain: Some surprises hide in the kitchen: a potato is a swollen stem, not a root; a banana is a berry but a strawberry is not; a peanut is not a nut; and chocolate starts as the seeds of a tree.
explain: Plants even fight back: an onion makes a stinging gas when you cut it, and a chilli makes a chemical that tricks your mouth into feeling fire.
fact: Carrots and beetroot are roots, celery and asparagus are stems, lettuce and spinach are leaves, broccoli and cauliflower are flowers, and peas and beans are seeds.
fact: A potato is a swollen underground stem called a tuber, and its eyes are buds; a sweet potato really is a root.
fact: Green potatoes and potato sprouts contain a mild poison, so they should not be eaten.
fact: To a botanist a fruit is the part that holds the seeds, so tomatoes, cucumbers, peppers and pumpkins are fruits; in 1893 the US Supreme Court still ruled the tomato a vegetable for tax.
fact: Botanically, bananas, grapes and tomatoes are berries, while a strawberry, with its seeds on the outside, is not a true berry, and neither are raspberries and blackberries.
fact: A banana plant is a giant herb, not a tree; shop bananas have no proper seeds, so new plants grow from shoots, and almost all of them are one kind, the Cavendish, which makes them easy prey for one disease.
fact: Chocolate comes from cacao beans, the seeds of a tree from South and Central America now grown mostly in West Africa; the beans are fermented, dried, roasted and ground, and the Aztecs drank chocolate as a bitter drink.
fact: Rice grows in flooded fields called paddies in warm countries, and more than half of all people eat rice every day.
fact: Popcorn is a kind of maize with a hard shell and a little water inside; heat turns the water to steam, and the hard shell holds the steam in until it bursts the kernel.
fact: Maize was bred from a wild grass called teosinte in Mexico about 9,000 years ago.
fact: Cutting an onion breaks its cells and releases a gas that stings the eyes, tears wash it away, and chilling the onion first slows the gas.
fact: Chillies contain capsaicin, which makes the mouth feel heat; birds do not feel it and spread chilli seeds, and milk calms the burn better than water.
fact: Peanuts are seeds that grow in pods underground like peas and beans, and some people are badly allergic to them.
fact: Sugar comes from sugar cane, a giant grass, or from sugar beet, a root; too much sugar harms teeth because mouth bacteria eat it and make acid.
exercise: Which part of the plant is a carrot? | The root | The thick stem | The leaf stalk | The flower
exercise: Which part of the plant is broccoli? | A bunch of flower buds | A thick root | A seed | A fruit with seeds inside
exercise: What is a potato, botanically? | A swollen underground stem called a tuber | A root, just like a carrot | A fruit that grows underground on the roots | A seed in a pod
exercise: Why should you not eat green potatoes or their sprouts? | They contain a mild poison | They are not ripe enough to taste | They give you green teeth for a week | They are much too hard to cook
exercise: Why is a tomato a fruit to a scientist? | It holds the plant's seeds | It is sweet like an apple or a pear | It grows on a tree like a cherry | It is red and juicy like a strawberry
exercise: Which of these is a true berry to a botanist? | A banana | A strawberry | A raspberry | A blackberry
exercise: Why is a strawberry not a true berry? | Its seeds are on the outside | It grows too close to the ground | It is far too small to be a real berry | It is red and grows on the ground
exercise: What is a banana plant? | A giant herb, not a tree | A palm tree | A kind of grass that grows bananas underground | A climbing vine
exercise: Why could one disease threaten almost all shop bananas? | Nearly all are one kind, the Cavendish, grown from shoots | Bananas have no skin | All bananas in the shops grow on one small island in the sea | Bananas catch colds from people
exercise: Where does chocolate come from? | Roasted seeds of the cacao tree | The sweet sap of a palm tree | Brown cow's milk | A root like a potato, dug up
exercise: What is a paddy? | A flooded field where rice grows | A machine that pops corn kernels | A sack for flour | A bread oven
exercise: Why does popcorn pop? | Water inside turns to steam and bursts the kernel | Air from the hot pan pushes inside the kernel | The oil explodes | The seed starts to grow very fast, all at once
exercise: Why does cutting an onion make you cry? | Broken cells release a gas that stings the eyes | The smell makes you sad | Onion juice squirts straight into your eyes from the knife | Onions are too cold
exercise: What calms a chilli burn best? | Milk | Cold water | Fizzy drink | A pinch of salt
exercise: What is a peanut really? | A seed in a pod that grows underground | A nut that grows on a tall tree in hot lands | A small potato | A dried fruit
exercise: Where does sugar come from? | Sugar cane or sugar beet | Salt mines | Milk | Flour ground from wheat
exercise: Which wild grass was maize bred from? | Teosinte, in Mexico | Wheat, in Ukraine | Rice, in China | Bamboo, in India and China
test: In a Bugs Bunny cartoon Bugs crunches carrots all day. Which part of the plant is he eating? | The root | The stem | The fruit | The seed
test: «potato's a root, obvs, it's in the ground» What is right? | It is a swollen stem, and its eyes are buds | Yes, it is a root like a carrot or a parsnip | It is a fruit that fell off | It is a seed that never grew
test: «tomato in a fruit salad?? you're mad» Which is true? | A cook calls it a vegetable, a botanist calls it a fruit | It is only a vegetable to everyone, cooks and scientists alike | It is a berry, so it is sweet | It is a root
test: Count them: a lunchbox has 2 carrots, 3 celery sticks and 4 lettuce leaves. How many roots, stems and leaves? | 2 roots, 3 stems and 4 leaves | 5 roots, 0 stems and 4 leaves | 2 roots, 4 stems and 3 leaves | 9 leaves and no roots
test: What would happen if you put a popcorn kernel with a crack in its shell in the hot pan? | It would not pop well, as the steam escapes | It would pop twice as big | It would turn into a seed | It would melt
test: «mouth on fire!! gimme water quick» What would work better? | A glass of milk | More cold water, fast | A spoonful of chilli | Holding your breath
test: Why do chilli plants not mind birds eating them but put off mice? | Birds do not feel the burn and spread the seeds whole | Birds are allergic to mice | Mice spread seeds much better than birds, so chillies want mice | Birds eat only the leaves
test: «can we share peanuts with the class?» What should you check first? | Whether anyone is allergic to peanuts | Whether the peanuts are cold | Whether peanuts are really nuts | Whether the teacher likes salt on them
test: In a cartoon Popeye eats a tin of spinach and his muscles pop up at once. What is real? | Spinach is a healthy leaf, but no food makes muscles grow in seconds | Spinach makes muscles grow at once | Spinach is a root that makes you strong | Spinach is poisonous
test: «you put the onion in the fridge first, why» What does it do? | Chilling slows the stinging gas, so fewer tears | It makes the onion sweeter | It kills the onion | It keeps flies off it
test: «sugar's bad for teeth cos it's sharp» What is the real reason? | Mouth bacteria eat sugar and make acid | Sugar crystals cut the teeth like tiny knives | Sugar is too cold | Sugar turns into stones
test: Why do farmers plant new banana plants from shoots and not from seeds? | Shop bananas have no proper seeds | Banana seeds are poisonous | Seeds grow only in Mexico | Shoots are cheaper than water
exam: Identify the part of the plant constituted by the potato tuber. | A swollen underground stem bearing buds | A root of the same nature as the carrot | A fruit containing seeds | A modified leaf
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the botanical status of the tomato? | It is a fruit, being that part of the plant which contains the seeds | It is a root vegetable | It is a modified stem | It is a flower bud
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the bursting of the popcorn kernel. | Water within the kernel is converted into steam, which ruptures the shell | Air is drawn into the kernel by the oil | The seed germinates instantaneously | The shell dissolves upon heating
exam: Ascertain the agent which calms the burning sensation produced by capsaicin most effectively. | Milk | Water | Common salt | Sugar
